::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve the system y = x + 3 and y = 2x + 1. {
= (2, 5)#Correct. Set x + 3 = 2x + 1 to get x = 2, then y = 5.
~ (1, 4)#This point satisfies y = x + 3 but not y = 2x + 1.
~ (3, 6)#This point satisfies y = x + 3 but not the second equation.
~ (2, 4)#The x-value is correct, but y should be 5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which point solves 2x + y = 7 and x - y = 2? {
~ (1, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and fails the second equation.
= (3, 1)#Correct. Substituting gives 2(3)+1=7 and 3-1=2.
~ (2, 3)#This satisfies neither equation completely.
~ (4, -1)#This satisfies x - y = 5, not 2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y = x<sup>2</sup> and y = 4. {
~ (4, 16)#This point is on y = x<sup>2</sup> but not y = 4.
~ (0, 4)#This is on y = 4 but not y = x<sup>2</sup>.
= (-2, 4) and (2, 4)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup> = 4, so x = -2 or x = 2.
~ (2, 2)#This point is not on either equation as stated.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>What does an intersection point of two graphs represent in a system? {
~ A point that satisfies only the first equation#A system solution must satisfy every equation.
~ The y-intercept of both graphs#Intersections are not always y-intercepts.
~ A point where neither equation is true#That cannot be a system solution.
= A point that satisfies both equations#Correct. At an intersection, both equations have the same x- and y-values.
}

::ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L01_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.1; MLA.A2.SYS.2<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y = x + 1 and y = x<sup>2</sup> - 1. {
= (-1, 0) and (2, 3)#Correct. Set x + 1 = x<sup>2</sup> - 1, so x<sup>2</sup> - x - 2 = 0.
~ (0, 1) and (1, 0)#These points do not satisfy both equations.
~ (1, 2) only#This point satisfies y = x + 1 but not the quadratic.
~ No solution#The line and parabola intersect twice.
}

